President Obama's job speech in which he  proposed an American Jobs Act before the  joint sessions of Congress was an  impressive piece of oratory I thought he  did wonderfully in his presentation but  i think it was a little weak in terms of  the magnitude of the crisis we're going  through he gave the impression that this  act would solve the problems well it  would help some people but it's pretty  limited in the overall impact I believe  in it it's not really up to the  magnitude of the problem we have 14  million unemployed and there's a risk  that the economy is going to sink into  recession so we'll have even more  unemployed and there's a serious risk  that this problem is going to continue  for years what Obama did was brilliant  for a politician he talked about some  tax cuts which is a gift to the people  who are listening and some expenditure  programs and then he left to another day  to talk about how they would be financed  but clearly because of the debt Control  Act which he signed August to he can't  raise expenditures in fact there's a  super committee that has to cut  expenditures further so the net effect  is still going to be to reduce stimulus  so a certain things they sound very good  and I guess they argue for example he  proposed that people who hire some or  companies that hire someone who had been  unemployed for more than six months  would get a special tax break great but  they're going to have to raise taxes  somewhere else and who's that going to  hit so it's not a dramatic improvement  President Obama did talk about tax  increases for the rich and for closing  off corporate loopholes but those are  the aspects of his act that are least  likely to happen the more likely thing  to happen I suppose  will be a payroll tax cut but there will  be something else that offsets that  maybe cut back in some government  expenditure Medicare or something like  that which will offset that there is  some stimulus in this pack but it's  limited so the President's speech to a  joint session of Congress on the night  of September 8th was both a marker of  how severe the jobs crisis is and of how  significant the president's political  troubles are right now he really felt  like he had to take the reins put out a  bold vision a very strong plan and  challenge Congress to act on it and the  plan is large i mean it's 450 billion  dollars in terms of the amount of money  that's going to be spent in a single  year where it to where it to happen  that's it as large as any year of the  stimulus bill that the president pushed  for in 2009 but given the reaction to  that bill now with members of Congress  from the republican party basically  saying the stimulus bill failed we know  that the president faces a very tough  road in Congress so the the real  challenge for him is to create both  public pressure and political pressure  in Congress to really get this this this  bill passed and so the question we  shouldn't we should be asking isn't is  this the ideal jobs bill I can think of  lots of ways in which it could be  designed better i think it probably  needs to be bigger given the scale of  the jobs crisis we're facing right now i  think that it the payroll tax holiday  for employers is probably not going to  have a big effect i would put more into  infrastructure but the question we  should be asking isn't is this big  enough the question we should be asking  is how is the present going to get  something on this scale done and the  only way that's going to happen is if he  pushes aggressively in congress and  takes his message to the American people  and to me the the real puzzle for  understanding what's going on now is  that consistently for the last few years  Americans have been saying unemployment  the jobs crisis is a higher priority  than deficit reduction but all we've  heard about is deficit reduction you  know the president gave a nod to deficit  reduction by saying he was going to pay  for this bill in the future and if he  pays for it too quickly we could really  see ourselves you know robbing Peter to  pay Paul in the sense that we could  see the money pulled out of the economy  when it's still very fragile we don't  just face the risk of a double-dip  recession we faced a probability or at  least a possibility of serious economic  malaise over a long period of time so  the president really needs to to keep  his eye on the ball of doing something  now immediately and pushing the serious  deficit reduction into the future  particularly through cutting healthcare  costs but you know Americans have been  saying jobs is the priority and Congress  has been acting as if the deficit which  is a serious problem but a long-term  problem should come first so at least  we're now having a real discussion about  jobs and both parties are going to be  pressed to offer something in the run-up  to the 2012 election
 

